  "body": "PR #706 grew the GUI's \"Set WCS from features\": pick edges, vertices, faces and cylinder walls, say which axis each one sets, and the WCS lands on the fitted frame, undoable (#701). The MCP surface cannot do the equivalent. `set_wcs` (`crates/app/src/mcp/setup_tools.rs`) takes only an absolute origin or a stock-bbox point plus offset, and rotation is a raw unit quaternion - so an agent has to solve the frame maths itself from `list_features` output, and a wrong quaternion posts shifted toolpaths with no diagnostic.\n\nThe id-driven precedent already exists: `locate_part` mates part/fixture features **by `list_features` id** and fits the 3-2-1 itself; `set_operation_geometry` takes the same ids.\n\n**Proposal:** a tool (say `set_wcs_from_features`) taking the active setup and picks as `[{id, role}]` with `role` in `origin | x | y | z`, and:\n\n- runs the GUI's exact law - `pick::roles::align_roles` (`crates/app/src/pick/roles.rs`): Z, X, Y priority, first axis exact, second projected perpendicular, third derived to close the frame; origin anchored at the feature's natural centre; cylinder walls by covariance fit. No second frame law to drift;\n- returns refusals as MCP error strings, the same named ones the GUI says: too few picks, duplicate role, fewer than two axes, parallel axes (5 degree tilt rule), zero-length edge, unresolvable datum, stale feature id;\n- queues the existing `CommandKind::SetWcs` - no new command kind, one undoable step. The \"commands ship with an MCP tool\" rule is satisfied by the tool itself (as noted on #706).\n\nFiled at the reviewer's suggestion in the #706 review (https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/706): keep the agent surface level with the GUI. Related: #701, #709.\n",
